Q: Flutter fails with &quot;No suitable NDK found for target architecture&quot; error in old project but runs fine in new project A: <p>Can you share your both of your build.gradle file? The solution is more likely linked to your ndkVersion in your build.gradle (android/app). You should set the ndkVersion to based on what you need, personally I like to target the version 29, but it depends on your project needs. Here's my personal ndkVersion:</p>
<pre><code>&quot;29.0.14206865&quot;
</code></pre>

Q: Kotlin/Native/cinterop pointer to custom class A: <p>In Kotlin/Native, you cannot declare <code>var</code> with fields in classes that inherit from CPointed. Such classes should reflect the C‑structure in its original form, without unnecessary fields. Otherwise, the compiler will throw the error that you got.</p>
<p>Your version of the class will not work, because x and y are Kotlin fields, not fields of the C structure. They have nothing to do with the memory that <code>rawPtr</code> points to. The x and y values should be read/written from/to the memory that <code>rawPt</code>r is viewing, not stored in a Kotlin object.</p>
<p>Judging by your fragment with <code>memScoped</code> and <code>allocPointerTo&lt;Point&gt;()</code>, you are working with a C-structure in your own memory. Therefore, you must have a structure like:</p>
<pre><code>typedef struct {    
    int x;    
    int y;
} Point;
</code></pre>
<p>then your Kotlin class should look something like this:</p>
<pre><code>import kotlinx.cinterop.*

@ExternalSymbol
external class Point(rawPtr: NativePtr) : CPointed(rawPtr) {    
    val x: Int by value(offsetOf(Point::class, &quot;x&quot;)) // you can do this val x: Int by value(offsetOf&lt;Point&gt;(Point::x))    
    val y: Int by value(offsetOf(Point::class, &quot;y&quot;))
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here, x and y are not class fields, but delegates that read/write to memory at the required offsets. This is the correct representation of the C‑structure. In your case, <code>allocPointerTo&lt;Point&gt;()</code> calls the dot type created by cinterop for the C structure.</p>
<p>@ExternalSymbol - is an annotation that marks classes that have an external definition in C/C++ code. That indicates this is not an ordinary Kotlin class, but a reference to an external C‑structure (Point) external class ‑ indicates that the implementation of the class is outside Kotlin (in the C library). Inheriting from CPointed, the class gets rawPtr support.</p>
<p>value - these are delegate that read/write field of the structure by offset relative to the beginning of the structure.</p>
<p>offsetOf - calculates the offset of the x field inside the Point C structure (the string &quot;x&quot; is the name of the field in C).</p>
<p>You can use it either in the version that you have written, or:</p>
<pre><code>memScoped {    
    val testPtr = alloc&lt;Point&gt;().apply {        
        pointed.x = 123        
        pointed.y = 321    
    }

    val xValue = testPtr.pointed.x    
    val yValue = testPtr.pointed.y
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you have the above C-structure, then your <code>memScoped</code> option will work without writing the Point class in Kotlin. In Kotlin, you just use the type that cinterop generates. Pointed gives access to native memory, and the x and y fields are the usual properties of the generated type. In fact, this is the most type-safe way.</p>
<p>P.S.</p>
<p>but you can do without C. By writing something like this (but it's not always safe).</p>
<pre><code>
class PointView(private val ptr: CPointer&lt;Point&gt;) {
    var x: Int
        get() = ptr.pointed?.x ?: error(&quot;null pointer&quot;)
        set(value) { ptr.pointed?.x = value }

    var y: Int
        get() = ptr.pointed?.y ?: error(&quot;null pointer&quot;)
        set(value) { ptr.pointed?.y = value }
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://kotlinlang.org/api/core/kotlin-stdlib/kotlinx.cinterop/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Docs</a></p>
